                        "stem": "<p>Observe the following table and answer the questions:<br><br><br>'X' Agricultural Field: Usually cultivated in small land and requires intensive care of crops.<br><br><br>'Y' Agricultural Field: Activities are conducted with the participation of everyone in the community and the produced goods are distributed according to the agreement.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is a grain crop?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Crops that belong to the Poaceae (Gramineae) family and are cultivated for their edible starchy seeds are called grain crops. Examples include rice, wheat, maize, etc.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why are field crops important for Bangladesh? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Field crops are vital for Bangladesh because they ensure food security for the massive population. They provide the staple food (carbohydrates) and serve as a major source of income for farmers.<br><br><br>Additionally, many field crops like jute and sugarcane act as primary raw materials for industries and contribute significantly to the national GDP through exports. They also provide fodder for livestock.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is a grain crop?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Crops that belong to the Poaceae (Gramineae) family and are cultivated for their edible starchy seeds are called grain crops. Examples include rice, wheat, maize, etc.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why are field crops important for Bangladesh? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Field crops are vital for Bangladesh because they ensure food security for the massive population. They provide the staple food (carbohydrates) and serve as a major source of income for farmers.<br><br><br>Additionally, many field crops like jute and sugarcane act as primary raw materials for industries and contribute significantly to the national GDP through exports. They also provide fodder for livestock.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Explain the agricultural field marked 'X' in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The agricultural field marked 'X' in the stem refers to Horticultural Crops. Horticulture is the branch of agriculture that deals with the intensive cultivation of fruits, vegetables, flowers, spices, and plantation crops.<br><br><br>Key characteristics matching the stem include:<br><br>1. Cultivation in small land areas (like kitchen gardens or specialized plots).<br><br>2. Requirement for intensive care, including individual plant attention, frequent irrigation, and protection.<br><br>3. High perishability, requiring quick marketing or processing. In the context of Bangladesh, this includes winter vegetables and seasonal fruits.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Analyze the importance of the 'Y' field of the stem in the socio-economic development of Bangladesh.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The field marked 'Y' refers to Cooperative Farming. Its importance in the socio-economic development of Bangladesh is immense:<br><br><br>1. Resource Pooling: It allows small and marginal farmers to combine their fragmented land, capital, and labor to use modern machinery (tractors, power pumps) that they couldn't afford individually.<br><br><br>2. Increased Productivity: Collective management and the application of scientific methods lead to higher yields compared to individual traditional farming.<br><br><br>3. Elimination of Middlemen: Farmers can market their products directly as a group, ensuring better prices and profit distribution according to the agreement.<br><br><br>4. Poverty Alleviation: By reducing production costs and increasing income, it improves the living standards of the rural community, fostering social unity and financial stability.</div></div>"
