            {
                        "chapter": "Agri1 Chapter 1",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch1_cq2",
                        "stem": "<p>Rashed is a rice farmer. He is a pioneer farmer. He is well-aware of the latest agricultural information. Other farmers in the area easily obtain agriculture-related information from him. At the beginning of the season, a club house near his home was selected as a center for season-long training on rice cultivation under the supervision of the Department of Agricultural Extension. In that center, 50 male and female farmers of the area are given hands-on training on rice cultivation throughout the season.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is 'e-Agriculture'?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>e-Agriculture is a service through which agricultural information and services are delivered to farmers using electronic media and information technology (such as mobile phones, the internet, or radio).</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the necessity of agricultural education in Bangladesh.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Agricultural education is essential for transforming traditional farming into a modern, scientific, and profitable sector. Since Bangladesh is an ivory-tower agrarian economy, the majority of the population depends on it, making scientific knowledge crucial for food security.<br><br><br>Through agricultural education, farmers learn about high-yielding varieties, modern irrigation, integrated pest management, and the proper use of fertilizers. This education helps in climate adaptation and ensures the sustainable use of land, ultimately improving the socio-economic condition of the country.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is 'e-Agriculture'?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>e-Agriculture is a service through which agricultural information and services are delivered to farmers using electronic media and information technology (such as mobile phones, the internet, or radio).</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain the necessity of agricultural education in Bangladesh.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Agricultural education is essential for transforming traditional farming into a modern, scientific, and profitable sector. Since Bangladesh is an ivory-tower agrarian economy, the majority of the population depends on it, making scientific knowledge crucial for food security.<br><br><br>Through agricultural education, farmers learn about high-yielding varieties, modern irrigation, integrated pest management, and the proper use of fertilizers. This education helps in climate adaptation and ensures the sustainable use of land, ultimately improving the socio-economic condition of the country.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) To which source of obtaining agricultural information and services does Rashed in the stem belong? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Rashed, mentioned in the stem, belongs to the \"Pioneer Farmer\" (Agragami Krishak) source of obtaining agricultural information and services. Pioneer farmers act as local leaders and reliable primary sources of information for their peers.<br><br><br>In the stem, it is mentioned that Rashed is well-versed in the latest agricultural news and other local farmers easily seek advice from him. This is a classic characteristic of an advanced or pioneer farmer who adopts new technologies first and then disseminates that knowledge to the community through informal discussions.<br><br><br>Such farmers bridge the gap between agricultural scientists and general farmers. Because they are local residents, other farmers trust their practical success more than theoretical advice, making Rashed an effective informal extension agent in his locality.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) How can farmers get agricultural information and services from the center mentioned in the stem? Analyze.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The center mentioned in the stem refers to the Farmers' Field School (FFS), organized by the Department of Agricultural Extension (DAE). Farmers can obtain information and services from this center through a systematic, hands-on learning approach throughout the cropping season.<br><br><br>Firstly, Practical Training: Unlike classroom learning, farmers here learn \"by doing.\" In this center, 50 farmers are trained directly in the field, allowing them to identify pests, diseases, and nutrient deficiencies in real-time. This helps them make informed decisions for their own plots.<br><br><br>Secondly, Group Discussion and Problem Solving: The center serves as a hub for \"Farmer-to-Farmer\" communication. Farmers share their experiences and challenges, and under the guidance of sub-assistant agriculture officers, they find scientific solutions collectively. This builds their confidence and leadership skills.<br><br><br>Thirdly, Observation and Analysis: Farmers are taught to use the \"Agro-Ecosystem Analysis\" (AESA) method. They regularly visit the field, observe the condition of the soil and crops, and decide whether a particular intervention (like pesticide application) is actually necessary, which reduces production costs.<br><br><br>Lastly, Technology Demonstration: The center acts as a demonstration site for the latest rice cultivation technologies. By seeing the success of modern methods at the center, farmers are encouraged to adopt these practices in their own fields, leading to increased productivity and sustainable agricultural development in the region.</div></div>"
