                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Which field of agriculture does Crop-1 in the chart represent? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Crop-1 in the chart represents the Field Crops (মাঠ ফসল) sector of agriculture. This is evident from the examples provided: rice, jute, wheat, and mustard, which are typically grown on a large scale in open fields.<br><br><br>Field crops are characterized by being grown collectively rather than individually. They are usually seasonal crops that cover vast areas of land and require less intensive individual care per plant compared to horticultural crops. These crops form the staple food supply and provide major industrial raw materials for the country.<br><br><br>In the context of Bangladesh, this sector is the most vital for national food security. Most field crops have a definite growing season and are harvested all at once. They are generally less perishable than horticultural crops, allowing them to be stored and transported over long distances to meet the caloric needs of the entire population.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Between Crop-1 and Crop-2 mentioned in the chart, which one is more profitable to cultivate? Show arguments in favor of your answer.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Between Crop-1 (Field Crops) and Crop-2 (Horticultural Crops), Crop-2 is generally more profitable from a commercial and economic standpoint.<br><br><br>Higher Economic Value: Horticultural crops such as eggplant, tomato, and guava have a much higher market price per unit of production compared to staple field crops like rice or wheat. Since these are often considered high-value nutritional products, they provide a better return on investment for small-scale farmers.<br><br><br>Intensive Land Use: Field crops usually occupy the land for a long duration for a single harvest. In contrast, many horticultural crops have shorter life cycles or can be grown in multi-cropping systems. This allows farmers to utilize the same piece of land multiple times a year, ensuring a continuous flow of income rather than a one-time seasonal payout.<br><br><br>Nutritional and Industrial Demand: There is a constant and growing demand for fresh vegetables and fruits in urban markets. Additionally, these crops are the primary raw materials for the burgeoning food processing industry. Exporting high-quality horticultural products like guava or off-season tomatoes earns significantly more profit and foreign exchange than exporting raw grains.<br><br><br>Conclusion: While Crop-1 is indispensable for basic survival and food security, Crop-2 is the key to economic empowerment and poverty alleviation. For a land-scarce country like Bangladesh, shifting toward high-value horticultural cultivation is a more strategic and profitable approach for improving the socio-economic status of rural farmers.</div></div>"
