                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) At what pH value does soil become alkaline? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil becomes alkaline when its pH value is greater than 7.0. This occurs due to the accumulation of basic salts like calcium, magnesium, and sodium in the soil, which increases the concentration of hydroxyl ($OH^-$) ions.<br><br><br>In alkaline conditions, the availability of essential micronutrients like iron, manganese, and boron decreases, which can hinder plant growth. In the context of Bangladesh, alkaline soils are commonly found in coastal regions where seawater intrusion or low rainfall prevents the leaching of basic salts from the topsoil.</div></div>"
