                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) What impact will the research institute seen by Jewel and Kader have on meeting the country's food demand in the future? Analyze in light of the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The research institute (BRRI) will play a transformative role in meeting the future food demands of Bangladesh by ensuring a steady increase in production despite decreasing arable land and a growing population.<br><br><br>Ensuring Food Security: With sixteen crore people to feed, the development of high-yielding varieties is a mechanical necessity. The stem mentions that most of the 62 varieties developed are high-yielding; these varieties produce significantly more grain per hectare than traditional ones. This increased productivity is the only way to sustain national self-sufficiency in rice, the country's primary staple food.<br><br><br>Nutritional Security: The future impact of BRRI extends beyond just quantity to the quality of food. By developing rice enriched with protein and zinc, as noted in the stem, the institute is addressing the \"hidden hunger\" or micronutrient deficiencies prevalent in the country. This ensures that the future generation is not only fed but also healthy, which is crucial for long-term national development.<br><br><br>Climate Resilience and Sustainability: As environmental challenges like salinity and floods increase, the institute’s role in breeding stress-tolerant rice varieties becomes vital. By providing farmers with seeds that can survive extreme weather, BRRI minimizes the risk of total crop failure. This stability in production protects the national economy from the need for expensive food imports and keeps market prices stable for the general population.<br><br><br>Conclusion: Therefore, the scientific advancements made by this institute act as the backbone of Bangladesh's agricultural resilience. As Jewel and Kader believe, the continuous innovation in rice genetics and farming technology will be the key factor in feeding the nation's millions in the decades to come. The institute’s ongoing support transforms traditional farming into a modern, science-based industry capable of meeting any future crisis.</div></div>"
