                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Analyze the final statement of Rafiz's uncle mentioned in the above stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The statement made by Rafiz's uncle, \"The cultivation of horticultural crops is more profitable,\" is highly accurate, logically justified, and reflects the modern economic realities of commercial agriculture.<br><br><br>Firstly, horticultural crops (such as fruits, off-season vegetables, and flowers) command a much higher market price per unit of land compared to traditional field crops like rice or wheat. Since these crops often cater to daily dietary needs, nutritional trends, and urban luxury demands, their market value remains consistently high, allowing smallholders to maximize cash inflows from limited land holdings.<br><br><br>Secondly, horticulture allows for advanced practices like multi-tier cropping, greenhouse farming, and off-season production. Producing vegetables or fruits before or after their natural seasonal peak allows farmers to sell their yield at premium rates when market supply is low, generating exponentially higher profit margins than staple grains that have fixed or controlled pricing.<br><br><br>In conclusion, although horticultural farming involves higher input costs, structural management, and perishability risks, its exceptionally high yield potential and market demand yield a significantly better cost-benefit ratio. Therefore, shifting from subsistence field cropping to high-value horticultural production is a proven strategy for accelerating rural prosperity, making his uncle's assertion entirely correct.</div></div>"
