                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain what 'Farmers Field School' (Krishok Math Skool) is.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Farmers Field School' (FFS) is a participatory, field-based training approach where a group of local farmers meet regularly throughout a crop cycle to learn sustainable farming practices directly from hands-on observation. It operates on the principle of \"learning by doing\" rather than conventional classroom lecturing.<br><br><br>The primary goal of this school is to empower farmers to become experts in their own fields. Through FFS, farmers learn advanced topics such as Integrated Pest Management (IPM), soil fertility conservation, balanced fertilizer usage, and climate-smart irrigation. This collective learning process enhances local decision-making and helps reduce reliance on costly chemical inputs.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) Write the names of two agricultural research institutions in Bangladesh.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Two prominent agricultural research institutions in Bangladesh are the Bangladesh Agricultural Research Institute (BARI) and the Bangladesh Rice Research Institute (BRRI).</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Explain what 'Farmers Field School' (Krishok Math Skool) is.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Farmers Field School' (FFS) is a participatory, field-based training approach where a group of local farmers meet regularly throughout a crop cycle to learn sustainable farming practices directly from hands-on observation. It operates on the principle of \"learning by doing\" rather than conventional classroom lecturing.<br><br><br>The primary goal of this school is to empower farmers to become experts in their own fields. Through FFS, farmers learn advanced topics such as Integrated Pest Management (IPM), soil fertility conservation, balanced fertilizer usage, and climate-smart irrigation. This collective learning process enhances local decision-making and helps reduce reliance on costly chemical inputs.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) How can Naim and his father benefit from the said university?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The university mentioned in the stem is Bangladesh Agricultural University (BAU). Naim and his father can benefit enormously from this pioneer institution across academic, practical, and informational spectrums.<br><br><br>For Naim, the institution provides a world-class academic platform to pursue professional higher education in various specialized fields like Agronomy, Veterinary Science, Fisheries, and Agricultural Engineering. Graduating from here will equip him with the scientific insight and credentials necessary to build a rewarding career as an agricultural officer, researcher, or modern entrepreneur.<br><br><br>For his father, who is an experienced farmer, the university acts as a repository of modern technological innovation. By exploring its research plots, nurseries, and technology extension hubs, he can gain direct access to newly developed high-yielding or stress-tolerant crop seedlings, advanced cattle rearing methodologies, and cost-efficient farm machinery. This direct knowledge transfer will allow him to upgrade his traditional field methods, cut down production costs, and exponentially increase his farm's commercial profitability.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Explain what contribution Naim's father can make to the agriculture of Bangladesh.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>As an experienced farmer who has now gained direct exposure to modern agricultural sciences at Bangladesh Agricultural University, Naim's father can make monumental contributions to the country's agrarian economy at the grassroots level.<br><br><br>1. Adoption and Dissemination of Innovation: By being a progressive cultivator, he can act as a bridge between scientific research and field application. He can set up practical demonstration plots in his village to test new climate-resilient varieties and mechanical harvesters, proving their efficiency to conservative neighbors.<br><br><br>2. Leading Local Knowledge Exchange: Using his extensive practical background combined with newly gathered institutional concepts, he can actively lead local farmer groups or informal community discussions. He can guide fellow smallholders on precise fertilizer ratios, biological pest control, and efficient water conservation, accelerating the modernization of his entire village.<br><br><br>3. Promoting Commercial Diversification: Instead of focusing strictly on traditional low-value grains, Naim's father can pioneer high-value commercial horticulture, advanced fish farming, or integrated dairy operations. This diversification not only maximizes his personal income but inspires a shift toward market-driven agribusiness among nearby communities, boosting rural employment and local food security.</div></div>"
