                        "chapter": "Dhaka &middot; 2025",
                        "question_id": "agr1_ch2_cq1",
                        "stem": "<p>The crisis of irrigation water in Bangladesh is increasing day by day. Therefore, for crop production, it is necessary to conserve irrigation water in various ways in water bodies including canals and wetlands (khals and beels). This water can be used later to provide irrigation to crop fields using various methods. Among the various methods of water irrigation, there is a widely practiced irrigation method. In this method, irrigation is provided in the form of flooding to crop fields where seeds have been sown in rows or broadcasted.</p>",
                        "knowledge_comprehension": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is soil erosion?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil erosion is the natural or human-induced process by which the fertile topsoil layer is detached and transported from one place to another by agents like water flow, wind, or gravitational force.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is it necessary to know the soil pH? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil pH indicates the chemical condition of the soil by measuring its acidity or alkalinity. Knowing the soil pH is critical because it directly influences the availability of essential plant nutrients; for instance, at extremely low or high pH levels, vital minerals become chemically locked and unavailable to plants.<br><br><br>Furthermore, soil pH dictates the activity of beneficial soil microorganisms responsible for decomposing organic matter. Since different crops require specific pH ranges to thrive, analyzing the pH allows farmers to apply correct amounts of lime or sulfur to neutralize the soil, ensuring maximum fertilizer efficiency and optimum crop yield.</div></div>"
                        ],
                        "full_cq": [
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(a) What is soil erosion?</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil erosion is the natural or human-induced process by which the fertile topsoil layer is detached and transported from one place to another by agents like water flow, wind, or gravitational force.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(b) Why is it necessary to know the soil pH? Explain.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>Soil pH indicates the chemical condition of the soil by measuring its acidity or alkalinity. Knowing the soil pH is critical because it directly influences the availability of essential plant nutrients; for instance, at extremely low or high pH levels, vital minerals become chemically locked and unavailable to plants.<br><br><br>Furthermore, soil pH dictates the activity of beneficial soil microorganisms responsible for decomposing organic matter. Since different crops require specific pH ranges to thrive, analyzing the pH allows farmers to apply correct amounts of lime or sulfur to neutralize the soil, ensuring maximum fertilizer efficiency and optimum crop yield.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Explain the irrigation method mentioned in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The irrigation method mentioned in the stem is Flood Irrigation (or Surface Flooding). This is a traditional, widely used method where water is directed from a source and allowed to flow freely across the entire surface of the cultivated field like a natural flood.<br><br><br>This method is highly suitable for flat, leveled lands and is commonly applied to close-growing crops or crops that have been broadcasted or sown in dense rows, such as rice, wheat, and jute. Water enters the field through a main channel and spreads evenly across the plots, soaking into the root zone over time.<br><br><br>While flood irrigation requires low initial setup costs, low technical skills, and is efficient for handling large volumes of water during peak seasons, it suffers from high water loss due to deep percolation and evaporation. If the land is not perfectly leveled, it can lead to waterlogging in low spots and underwatering on higher ridges, emphasizing the need for controlled management.</div></div>",
                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(d) Analyze how water can be conserved in water bodies to tackle the irrigation water crisis in Bangladesh, in light of the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The stem addresses the growing irrigation water crisis in Bangladesh and highlights the necessity of conserving water in surface water bodies like canals, rivers, and natural wetlands (khals and beels). To tackle this crisis effectively, several systematic approaches can be implemented to store water during the rainy season for use during dry periods.<br><br><br>1. Dredging and Excavation: Over time, siltation reduces the water-holding capacity of local canals and wetlands. Excavating and re-dredging these water bodies increases their depth and storage capacity, allowing them to retain vast volumes of rainwater that would otherwise run off into the sea, ensuring a dependable source for dry-season flood irrigation.<br><br><br>2. Constructing Sluice Gates and Rubber Dams: Installing controlled water-control structures across connection canals helps manage water flow. Closing these gates at the end of the monsoon traps water within upstream channels, creating localized reservoirs that farmers can draw from without depleting groundwater tables.<br><br><br>3. Community-Based Integrated Watershed Management: Implementing joint management systems among local communities ensures equitable water distribution and prevents the pollution of water bodies. By utilizing these conserved surface water reservoirs for techniques like the flood irrigation described in the stem, Bangladesh can significantly reduce its reliance on over-extracted underground aquifers, promoting sustainable and climate-resilient agricultural growth.</div></div>"
