                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Explain the reason why the pH value of Abdul Kuddus's second plot of land is 8.5.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The pH value of 8.5 in Abdul Kuddus's second plot of land indicates that the soil is highly alkaline. In Bangladesh, soil alkalinity is primarily caused by environmental factors, excessive chemical inputs, and geographical locations where basic soluble salts accumulate in the root zone.<br><br><br>The primary cause for this high pH value is the accumulation of basic cations such as calcium ($Ca^{2+}$), magnesium ($Mg^{2+}$), and sodium ($Na^+$) in regions with low rainfall or brackish groundwater irrigation. When these basic salts are not leached deep into the subsoil due to poor drainage or dry climatic conditions, they dominate the soil exchange complex, forcing the hydrogen ion concentration down and pushing the pH upward past the neutral mark.<br><br><br>Additionally, the repeated use of alkaline irrigation water or basic fertilizers without adding sufficient organic matter accelerates salt accumulation. This high alkaline state restricts the solubility of essential micronutrients like iron, zinc, and manganese, making it highly detrimental for standard rice cultivation and necessitating specialized reclamation treatments.</div></div>",
