                                    "<div class='cq-part mb-4'><p class='font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200'>(c) Describe the irrigation management system mentioned in the stem.</p><div class='mt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300'>The irrigation management system described in the stem refers to the Alternate Wetting and Drying (AWD) method, which is identified by the vertically inserted perforated pipes in a field without standing water. This is an advanced, water-saving technology designed specifically for sustainable rice production.<br><br><br>Structure and Installation: In this method, a perforated plastic pipe (usually 30 cm long and 7–10 cm in diameter) is buried vertically into the soil so that 20 cm is below the surface and 10 cm remains above. The soil inside the pipe is removed so that the water level beneath the soil surface becomes clearly visible from the top.<br><br><br>Water Regulation Mechanism: Irrigation is not applied continuously; instead, the field is allowed to dry until the water level inside the pipe drops to 15 cm below the soil surface. Once it reaches this threshold, the field is irrigated again to a depth of about 5 cm, and this cyclical wetting and drying process is maintained until the flowering stage of the rice crop, significantly conserving water resources.</div></div>",
